{"kind":"report","text":"Exact-head review of b013d6480215c56333e1130f4292e10d79c3766d, resting on all 24 artifacts, packages/git/README.md first. Changes requested.\n\nArchitecture: the preview and landing now use one deterministic planner with fixed (main, head, lane, generation), and the clean preview supplies the exact integration object. The new log remote uses the existing gateway and lease push; its outcome mapping keeps unresolved publication unresolved. The local real-L/real-Git test and dated live results support exact three-commit publication, read-back, object reconstruction and log verification. Two boundary gaps remain.\n\nP2 — require next to be a commit before pushing (src/publisher/gitops.ts:465). rev-list --parents -n 1 succeeds with empty output for a tree or blob. With a null lease that matches the required empty parent list, and rev-list --objects also succeeds. Two independent local real-Git diagnostics passed a valid decoded request whose next was respectively the empty tree and empty blob: both moved refs/artroom/log to the non-commit object and returned {ok:true}. This violates the README's explicit commit requirement and leaves no verifiable log history. Check the exact object's type before any push, retain exact parent/connectivity checks, and cover both object types with an unchanged remote ref on refusal.\n\nP2 — bound the transfer rather than the accumulated log (src/publisher/log-push.ts:61–88 with packages/log/src/publisher.ts:153–157,222–237). The 64 MiB / 100,000-object request cap is applied to everything L supplies. L's real publisher builds and sends the complete current tree, including retained objects already reachable from the lease, on each publication. The real golden log sent 7,154 / 15,029 / 15,562 bytes; publications two and three resent 2,502 / 4,508 bytes. A further independent test scaled only the byte limit down to exercise the actual decoder: the full later request was refused, while its new-object subset was accepted, landed the same computed commit through GitOps.pushLog, and verifyLog passed. This is a small-limit reproduction, not a 64 MiB load run. As retained content and full segments grow, ordinary small additions eventually cannot publish under the fixed full-tree cap; repeated retries cannot settle the pending cohort. Use incremental objects relative to the exact lease and/or bounded staged transfer with durable retry handling, keeping the final complete commit and lease fence. Raising the cap alone does not preserve the intended complete, scalable scope. Cover an accumulated log larger than a single bounded transfer, a small next cohort, restart/retry and independent verification.\n\nSecurity: only the log ref is admitted, request data is decoded and bounded before GitOps, the gateway fences the exact update, object data is written through stdin, and only a confirmed outcome is success. Parent fetching and lease rejection preserve another writer's ref. The non-commit case is the missing object-type check above. New preview objects go through the dedicated object ref fence; repository code is not run. Read-by-type fallbacks must still require the reconstructed object to hash to the requested SHA and fail unavailable if all readers fail. A token's expiry or revocation still does not establish non-application.\n\nSimplification: sharing the planner and publication machinery is sound. Keep one commit serialization and one final publication fence; avoid a special recovery path that rebuilds a different commit. Filter or stage redundant object transfer instead of imposing a maximum lifetime log size.\n\nValidation: clean root strict type check; 126 Git Node tests, eight workerd SQLite tests and all three real-L/real-Git publication tests passed. The three independent diagnostic cases passed, demonstrating the type defect and transfer issue. All 24 changed artifacts and all dated result values were reviewed against approved B6; the isolated candidate worktree is clean after saving diagnostics under /tmp/artroom-checker-pushlog-diagnostics. Live drivers were read, not executed; no deployment, network Git push, token mint or remote cleanup was performed by checker.\n\nIntegration remains full scope: lane A must adopt the new pushLog route, readLogRef instead of binding.log for this non-branch ref, tolerant typed object reads with exact SHA validation, preview for disjoint non-fast-forward cases with integration, and lane/generation planner inputs. The dated harness evidence exposes these needs; it does not substitute for actual Room admission/check/landing/verified-log end-to-end coverage.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d4d93c31aedeed148ff33f2d9a5d21d68a1bd529","binding":"assigned","head":"b013d6480215c56333e1130f4292e10d79c3766d","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c6625da70715bea7f989ee4af8822227e85f5db7\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b84fbd41f42360fc1fc5dea2c976410e7497a150\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c77b3003ce0ad6003894e3452cf3c2aa1a7584b2","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}